# SO-03 access consolidation — maintained completion ledger

**Updated:** 30 September 2026. **State:** implementing in the preserved, uncommitted KDPS working tree. Final acceptance remains blocked. Current commands, outcomes and the tested content identity are maintained in [access evidence](so-03-access-evidence.md).

## Approved scope and safeguards

SO-03 owns one server-side evaluator, scoped role assignments, versioned role/workflow policy, stable brand identity needed for access, canonical access administration and access enforcement across active delivery channels. SO-04–SO-14 and SO-16–SO-20 retain business retirement ownership; SO-15 and SO-21 verify consolidation milestones. SBU retirement stays disabled until SO-04.

Owner and Admin may administer policy within authorised scope. Administration does not grant protected business fields. Personal assignment changes are refused; shared-policy changes may benefit the changer after step-up and session invalidation. A reviewer must be a different human and have independent authority before the change; an authorised beneficiary may review. Follow-ups use the existing working calendar, not elapsed calendar hours.

Assignments retain their site/brand tuples and stable IDs. Explicit all includes future members; selected scope remains fixed and empty scope grants nothing. Missing workflow actions stay disabled until an explicit versioned upgrade. Name matches are reconciliation suggestions only; trusted tenant-owned relationships or reviewed mappings establish brand identity.

## Implemented continuation slices

- Scoped helpers require explicit section/rung parameters and fail closed when absent. Legacy active context values must be stable site/brand IDs.
- Specialist access gates now use named, stored role steps and `AccessContext` scope/field demands. Existing roles receive no automatic step top-up; the policy editor offers defaults for explicit review.
- Staff retirement, future assignment editing/removal, scheduled session expiry, direct self-assignment refusal, last-administrator continuity and independent review provenance have targeted denial tests.
- Stable brand references and append-only bindings preserve business snapshots. Mapping rejects stale, foreign and conflicting sources. Immutable ledger identity can follow a proven booking relationship. Existing foreign references cannot be silently rebound.
- Goods scan candidates and sale/report writers carry proven brand IDs. A printed bill whose source remains unresolved is preserved without inventing brand identity; brand-scoped readers cannot obtain it, and reconciliation remains required.
- Alert inbox/history uses stable brand identity or explicit whole-site scope. Protected financial/customer/employee titles require fields in the same qualifying assignment. The alert job validates tenant source ownership before writes, preserves established links and carries known brand IDs. Whole-site alert snapshots cannot be mapped to one brand. The seen cursor writes only the caller’s timestamp under a live session/security lock, with Home authority rechecked before commit.
- The scheduled browser journey edits and removes a real backend future assignment, checking the same ID and exact start. Disposable proof setup explicitly versions missing section policy and the Owner staff step through canonical password-confirmed policy endpoints; production evaluation has no fallback.

- Logout waits for server revocation before clearing the local session and navigating. A failed request remains visible for retry; delayed acknowledgement and immediate reload are covered by real-backend browser regressions.

