# SO-03 access consolidation — completion plan

## Context

SO-03 must end with one server-side permission authority, applied the same way on every channel. That authority is:

- versioned role and workflow policy;
- effective-dated role assignments, each carrying its own site/brand scope by stable ID;
- additive step rules.

Legacy access bridges must also be retired and assignment migration reconciled.

The preserved, uncommitted working tree contains an interrupted implementation (HEAD `b9e1e25`, 187 changed paths; most SO-03 code and all SO-03 tests are untracked). Its own evidence already says SO-03 is **not complete**.

**This investigation.** It was read-only and static:

- 3 parallel trace agents reviewed the code.
- I spot-checked each high-severity claim in source myself.
- The only checks re-run on this tree were `check_so03_consolidation.py --check` (2,908 surfaces match) and `check_so03_boundaries.py` (pass). Every other green result is historical. No tests, migrations or database probes were run.
- Line numbers below refer to the current working tree.

**Headline.** The ledger's 88 frozen references understate the problem. The main risks sit **outside** that list:

- a union-based section gate plus a hidden per-request `_required_section` stamp that the legacy scoping helpers depend on;
- 22 hand-kept role-code gates;
- name-based brand predicates on about 30 text `brand` columns. Brand names are not unique within a tenant, and a Brand Manager can rename their own brand to another brand's name, which is an **escalation**;
- cost and customer fields returned with no field check;
- admin-policy lockout and escalation gaps.

SO-03 cannot be accepted on the current tree.

