## 1. Intended target

Sources: roadmap SO-03; main PRD §4.2, §4.3, §10.1–10.6, §15.2 (step-up), §15.3 OQ-28 and Appendix B; store PRD §4, §5.1, ST-OPS-5, §33.2 and §34; register rows R-CTL-003, R-CTL-004 and R-QLT-006.

| ID | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| T1 | **Six roles** (Owner, Store Person, Warehouse, Brand Manager, Accounts, Admin = `it_admin`) are initial *configurable* defaults. They use the B.4 grid and the B.3 ladder (none < view < operate < approve < manage). A new role starts with no authority. |
| T2 | **One authority:** versioned role policy (section levels plus protected fields), effective-dated scoped assignments and versioned step rules. There is no second grant system and no lasting bridge. |
| T3 | **Scope per assignment**, by stable site/brand IDs, never names. "All" includes future members; a selected set stays fixed; an empty scope grants nothing. Actions, fields and scope never combine across assignments. Every site×brand cell of a multi-row operation must be covered by one qualifying assignment. |
| T4 | **Role and workflow policy** are editable without code, with step-up, versioned, and bounded by fixed safeguards: tenant isolation, field ceilings, workflow floors, approval limits and separate-person rules. |
| T5 | **Protected-field defaults (§10.4)** apply to reads *and* writes on API, search, exports, files, events and till datasets. Data is never sent and then hidden. A cost-basis request without authority gets an explicit field-denied answer. Warehouse sees cost only on its own PTs. Admin has no Money access. History shows "Restricted person" where the reader may not see the actor. |
| T6 | **Separate-person approvals:** the preparer, maker or row reviewer never approves; the drafter or reporter never confirms their own item; approval binds the exact inputs and policy version. |
| T7 | **Step-up** is required for staff retire, login changes, role assignments, role/workflow policy and privileged review. It is not required for staff create/edit. |
| T8 | An **authority change invalidates** affected sessions when it takes effect. A password change ends sessions. A must-change-password session is restricted. |
| T9 | Each privileged change creates an **Owner-owned follow-up**, due within one working day, closed only by a *different* authorised person. |
| T10 | **Login creation** confirms identity and email ownership; the admin sets a temporary password; resets are assisted. |
| T11 | People, login, policy and privileged-change **history** is scoped, newest-first and paginated. |
| T12 | **Step rules (§4.3)** are additive. PT approver is configurable (office, warehouse or either, plus an optional second check). |
| T13 | **Export, restore and monitoring** need Owner or Admin with step-up. An Admin export omits Money. |
| T14 | **X-PLT** is not tenant Admin. Service identities have their own scope and audit identity. |
| T15 | **Manager override** uses a personal, hashed PIN, and the approving identity is retained. |
| T16 | **One versioned `/api/auth/me`.** The server returns `allowed_actions`. Client hints never authorise. One People & Access editor; tested bookmark redirects. |
| T17 | The **same evaluator on every channel**: UI/API, search/count, imports, exports, files/print, events/reconnect, queued jobs and till sync. Delayed delivery re-checks authority. |
| T18 | **Safe assignment migration:** preserve historical grants, approval evidence and identity links. Never map an unsupported responsibility to Owner. |
| T19 | **Retire old authority:** `RoleGrant` runtime, `User.role/scope_type/stores/brands`, actor policies, role-code gates, superuser shortcuts. CI blocks their return. |
| T20 | Every remaining duplicate has an owned replacement or retirement item (C01–C20). |

